     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Confirming it doesn't change the safety problem. 1000 mg of sertraline once daily is five times the maximum labeled dose, and I don't know of any clinical indication that would justify it. Giving it could seriously harm your patient.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose right away.** For example, 50 mg or 100 mg by mouth once daily, or whatever dose your team intends.\n- **Enter the order with your attending's or resident's input.** If the team really does want a dose above the labeled maximum, the supervising prescriber should place or co-sign it. They can document the rationale, and pharmacy can review it.\n- **Check the dose with pharmacy.** They can help if you're unsure what was intended, for example if 1000 mg came from a transcription error or a unit mix-up.\n\nIf your patient may already have taken a very large amount of sertraline, that's an overdose. It needs urgent clinical evaluation and poison control, not a routine order.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
